  "narrative": {
    "intro": "The Department of Defense has obligated $298B in contracts FY2026 to date. The Department of Defense recorded 2,473,722 awards with an average award size of $120K. Obligations fell 39.5% year over year. Fiscal history shows obligations of $387B in FY21, $414B in FY22, $457B in FY23, $446B in FY24, and $492B in FY25, followed by $298B in FY26. The concentration of FY2026 obligations is visible in manufacturing and technical services, led by Aircraft Manufacturing ($36.7B), Guided Missile and Space Vehicle Manufacturing ($26.5B), and Engineering Services ($26.2B). The Department of Defense is also contracting in research, ship building, health insurance, and construction categories. Top vendor obligations in FY2026 to date include LOCKHEED MARTIN CORPORATION ($11.5B), ELECTRIC BOAT CORPORATION ($10.8B), a second listing for LOCKHEED MARTIN CORPORATION ($10.6B), and RAYTHEON COMPANY ($8.2B). This snapshot reflects partial, fiscal year to date totals and shows where the Department of Defense is currently directing contract dollars.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How do current obligations compare to prior years?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense obligated $298B in FY26 to date, down 39.5% year over year. The Department of Defense recorded obligations of $492B in FY25, $446B in FY24, $457B in FY23, $414B in FY22, and $387B in FY21. The Department of Defense recorded 2,473,722 awards so far in FY26 with an average award size of $120K. These figures are partial because FY2026 totals are fiscal year to date."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which industries dominate FY2026 to date?",
        "body": "Aircraft Manufacturing leads FY2026 obligations with $36.7B. The Department of Defense obligated $26.5B to Guided Missile and Space Vehicle Manufacturing and $26.2B to Engineering Services. The Department of Defense also obligated $21B to Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences, $18.4B to Ship Building and Repairing, and $13B to Direct Health and Medical Insurance Carriers."
      }
    ]
  },
  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "How much does Department of Defense spend on contracts?",
      "a": "The Department of Defense has obligated $298B in contracts FY2026 to date. The Department of Defense recorded 2,473,722 awards with an average award size of $120K. Obligations are down 39.5% year over year. These totals are partial because they are fiscal year to date."
    },
    {
      "q": "What does Defense buy?",
      "a": "Defense buys major manufacturing, technical, research, health, and construction services. Aircraft Manufacturing received $36.7B, Guided Missile and Space Vehicle Manufacturing received $26.5B, Engineering Services received $26.2B, Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences received $21B, and Ship Building and Repairing received $18.4B. The Department of Defense also obligated $13B to Direct Health and Medical Insurance Carriers and funds for construction and instrument manufacturing categories."
    },
    {
      "q": "Who are Defense's largest contractors?",
      "a": "Defense's largest contractors by obligations include LOCKHEED MARTIN CORPORATION ($11.5B), ELECTRIC BOAT CORPORATION ($10.8B), a second listing for LOCKHEED MARTIN CORPORATION ($10.6B), RAYTHEON COMPANY ($8.2B), and THE BOEING COMPANY ($5.9B). Additional top vendors include HUMANA GOVERNMENT BUSINESS INC ($5.5B), a second listing for THE BOEING COMPANY ($5.2B), AMERISOURCEBERGEN DRUG CORP ($5B), TRIWEST HEALTHCARE ALLIANCE CORP ($4.7B), and RTX CORPORATION ($3.8B)."
